  {
   "type": "done",
   "answer": "No, hyper-threading is not equivalent to adding a fully independent physical core.\n\nAccording to the lecture, hyper-threading introduces Simultaneous Multithreading (SMT), where multiple hardware threads share a single physical core [S1, p. 29]. While each logical thread maintains its own architectural state, the core's execution units, caches, queues, and predictors are shared among these threads [S1, p. 29]. This contrasts with a fully independent physical core, which would have its own dedicated execution resources rather than sharing them with another thread.\n\nThe lecture further distinguishes this from the GPU architecture, which uses many separate, in-order, SIMD-pipeline cores (light cores) to handle thousands of threads, whereas the CPU uses a few heavy cores with hyper-threading to optimize latency for a small number of complex threads [S2, p. 43].",
   "citations": [],
